00:12.320 --> 00:14.640
for those of you that use those.

00:14.640 --> 00:16.400
I know that some of you swear by limiting.

00:16.400 --> 00:19.360
And you don't want to use anti-triggers.

00:19.360 --> 00:23.240
And if you have the risk reward for it,

00:23.240 --> 00:27.760
then you're profitable with it, then sure.

00:27.760 --> 00:31.480
I love limits myself as well.

00:31.480 --> 00:33.560
But knowing when to use anti-triggers

00:33.560 --> 00:37.680
and how they're going to essentially save your ass

00:37.680 --> 00:39.800
is definitely helpful.

00:39.800 --> 00:46.840
So this is from last week.

00:46.840 --> 00:50.640
This is a five-minute rejection block.

00:50.640 --> 00:52.600
And basically, what this is going to be about

00:52.600 --> 00:57.360
is this is a bit of a more advanced thing,

00:57.360 --> 01:00.520
but it's basically just scaling down in timeframes

01:00.520 --> 01:06.720
to see essentially what is inside of our five,

01:06.720 --> 01:09.920
in this case, 15-minute, rejection blocks.

01:09.920 --> 01:13.160
Now, we also have 10 AM here, so also something

01:13.160 --> 01:15.720
to take into consideration.

01:15.720 --> 01:19.000
Now, when I go from the five-minute,

01:19.000 --> 01:23.560
and I see that we have a rejection block here, how big is this?

01:23.560 --> 01:24.680
This is too big.

01:24.680 --> 01:30.200
This is 35 points.

01:30.200 --> 01:34.080
So it's too big to just enter off of the start of it

01:34.080 --> 01:39.040
and have your stop-offs at the low, right?

01:39.040 --> 01:43.640
Now, we already know how do we use these?

01:43.640 --> 01:46.640
The CE, right?

01:46.640 --> 01:51.240
The CE is oftentimes going to be the best option.

01:51.240 --> 01:56.680
Now, sometimes, let's say this would have been smaller.

01:56.680 --> 02:02.520
Let's say this would have been 15 points,

02:02.520 --> 02:05.920
or whatever this is, 20 points.

02:05.920 --> 02:08.880
And we have structure inside of this week

02:08.880 --> 02:15.840
that tells us that price wants to reverse here.

02:15.840 --> 02:18.080
You guys are familiar with IFEGs.

02:18.080 --> 02:22.280
You guys are familiar with in state of delivery, basically,

02:22.280 --> 02:26.280
just, let's say we're looking at points, right?

02:26.280 --> 02:35.000
We want to have a candle closure above a bearish candle

02:35.000 --> 02:37.400
for our change instead of delivery.

02:37.400 --> 02:40.760
Now, we get everything in one candle.

02:40.760 --> 02:46.000
We get one-minute rejection block, one-minute IFEG,

02:46.000 --> 02:48.400
and one-minute changes to delivery.

02:48.400 --> 02:52.840
And we basically just want to mark out

02:52.840 --> 02:57.000
these one-minute conferences inside of this five-minute

02:57.000 --> 03:01.880
conference, or five-minute entry trigger, right?

03:01.880 --> 03:08.520
So for this, we can see that we have an IFEG, a change

03:08.520 --> 03:11.840
in state of delivery, and a one-minute rejection block

03:11.840 --> 03:14.480
that has already been mitigated, which,

03:14.480 --> 03:18.760
in this case, is good for our five-minute.

03:18.760 --> 03:20.800
Let's fast forward.

03:20.800 --> 03:26.240
Now, this ends up hitting the CE on the first tap.

03:26.240 --> 03:29.240
This is a very choppy day.

03:29.240 --> 03:32.640
And then it comes back into it one more time,

03:32.640 --> 03:38.560
and it visits the IFEG on the one-minute.

03:38.560 --> 03:43.600
And then it just kind of does its thing, right?

03:43.640 --> 03:46.960
So this is one example of how you would play this.

03:46.960 --> 03:51.600
Now, if you are looking to enter on the second tap,

03:51.600 --> 03:55.600
and you want to use this, as a conference,

03:55.600 --> 03:58.160
you have these two overlapping, which is nice.

03:58.160 --> 04:04.640
Now, your trade, in this case, would look like this.

04:04.640 --> 04:08.840
You would use this low instead of this low.

04:08.840 --> 04:11.640
For risk-reward purposes, right?

04:11.640 --> 04:16.120
And then you just target a swing-high low posing

04:16.120 --> 04:20.040
structure on field balances, right?

04:20.040 --> 04:22.680
Nice little unfilled five right there,

04:22.680 --> 04:26.800
with some liquidity below, nice, whatever, right?

04:26.800 --> 04:32.040
So both of these trades are valid.

04:32.040 --> 04:36.200
You know, limit them, use one-minute entry triggers.

04:36.200 --> 04:37.840
I don't know if you get that here.

04:37.840 --> 04:42.640
It's too aggressive of a move, so this just kind of goes.

04:42.640 --> 04:43.840
But yeah, that's one example.

04:43.840 --> 04:50.440
Let's move on to yesterday.

04:50.440 --> 04:52.880
Actually, let me just delete everything.

04:52.880 --> 04:54.440
Let's move on to yesterday.

04:54.440 --> 05:04.920
So leave our replay, and let's take a look.

05:04.920 --> 05:16.280
Now, this is the trade that I was in that got edged, right?

05:16.280 --> 05:20.120
With this, this is an example of a five-minute rejection

05:20.120 --> 05:22.160
block that I would not take, because we do

05:22.160 --> 05:26.360
have relative equal highs, right?

05:26.360 --> 05:29.520
If we market, though, and we look at lower time frame,

05:29.520 --> 05:32.760
what do we have inside of this rejection block?

05:32.760 --> 05:35.800
We have a very similar story.

05:35.800 --> 05:41.760
We have a one-minute IFEG, and we have a one-minute change

05:41.760 --> 05:43.640
in state of delivery.

05:43.640 --> 05:46.680
Same thing as we had on the last one, right?

05:46.680 --> 05:50.800
Now, this would actually have needed to sweep that liquidity

05:50.800 --> 05:53.920
from me down, even consider taking this.

05:53.920 --> 05:55.720
But let's fast forward.

05:58.840 --> 06:02.560
This also, I believe, ends up tapping the CE.

06:02.560 --> 06:06.520
And then it ends up making another five-minute rejection

06:06.520 --> 06:07.520
block off of this.

06:10.000 --> 06:15.320
Now, let's delete this and scale down in time frames

06:15.320 --> 06:16.960
on this five-minute rejection block.

06:19.480 --> 06:22.440
What is there inside of this?

06:22.440 --> 06:30.080
Have a one-minute IFEG, and we have a one-minute changes

06:30.080 --> 06:33.720
in state of delivery.

06:33.720 --> 06:38.240
And these rejection blocks often, they look the same.

06:38.240 --> 06:41.840
And this is kind of like how you can differentiate

06:41.840 --> 06:45.000
between what's a good one and what's a bad one, right?

06:45.000 --> 06:47.440
The good rejection blocks on the five-minute,

06:47.440 --> 06:49.280
often, if you scale down into the one-minute,

06:49.280 --> 06:52.120
you'll have this one big candle that

06:52.120 --> 06:57.560
gives you three entry triggers on lower time frame,

06:57.560 --> 06:59.680
like all in one candle.

06:59.680 --> 07:02.600
And that is just called displacement.

07:02.600 --> 07:08.240
And that is very often going to be good, obviously,

07:08.240 --> 07:11.440
if it's in your desired direction.

07:11.440 --> 07:13.360
So this is kind of, for those of you

07:13.360 --> 07:16.840
that use entry triggers and know what to look for.

07:16.840 --> 07:20.880
This goes, obviously, as well, for like 15-minute.

07:20.880 --> 07:22.600
What you want to look for, like, where

07:22.600 --> 07:28.480
in the rejection block would you want to enter, ideally,

07:28.480 --> 07:32.400
right, what happened here?

07:32.400 --> 07:38.840
And this is basically just how to get even more precise

07:38.840 --> 07:41.000
with your entries.

07:41.000 --> 07:43.560
Obviously, when you aim for precision,

07:43.560 --> 07:45.560
you're going to run the risk of getting

07:45.560 --> 07:46.680
left out of the trade.

07:46.680 --> 07:51.560
So sometimes you want to have a very discounted entry.

07:51.560 --> 07:53.480
Price, it doesn't always want to go there,

07:53.480 --> 07:55.040
and it just leaves without you.

07:55.040 --> 07:57.880
So that's something you're going to have

07:57.880 --> 08:01.360
to make up your mind on.

08:01.360 --> 08:05.000
Do you want the high risk reward, like the highest possible?

08:05.000 --> 08:08.400
Or do you just want the best odds of getting into the trade

08:08.400 --> 08:13.720
and catching like a nice bread and butter, 1 to 5, 1 to 6?

08:13.720 --> 08:15.520
Completely up to you, also depends

08:15.520 --> 08:17.320
on what kind of account you're trading.

08:17.320 --> 08:20.200
So also like your psychology, what

08:20.200 --> 08:24.360
you prefer, what's easier on your mental, all these things.

08:24.360 --> 08:26.680
I can't decide that for you.

08:26.680 --> 08:30.080
Some use swear by limits, some use swear by entry figures.

08:30.080 --> 08:31.440
I use both.

08:31.440 --> 08:34.960
They perform well in different scenarios.

08:34.960 --> 08:38.560
For example, today, I used, or sorry, yesterday,

08:38.560 --> 08:41.680
I used a 1 minute entry trigger because we were so close

08:41.680 --> 08:42.840
to market open.

08:42.840 --> 08:44.640
So I just wanted some confirmation

08:44.640 --> 08:47.160
to make sure that I didn't get absolutely

08:47.160 --> 08:50.120
molested right at market open.

08:50.120 --> 08:56.720
So yeah, hopefully that was helpful to kind of validate,

08:56.720 --> 08:59.720
if you will, your entry triggers.

